WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.842 --> 00:00:02.825
Cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin

00:00:02.825 --> 00:00:04.780
are terrible for the environment.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:07.540
It's an extremely inefficient way

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.870
of conducting transactions.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.850
It's a way to both hide dirty money

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.840
and destroy the environment
at the same time.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.750
The environmental attacks on Bitcoin

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.730
are best understood as a
strategy by economic media

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:20.980
and political elites to undermine

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.250
a form of money they can't control.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.120
Critics distort the basic facts

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.510
about what's known as Bitcoin mining,

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.540
the process through which a
global network of computers

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.420
maintain the Bitcoin
network through computation.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.610
Though energy-intensive,

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.800
this process is what makes Bitcoin

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.658
a truly decentralized monetary system.

00:00:39.658 --> 00:00:42.150
The amount of energy that's consumed

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.730
in processing those
transactions is staggering.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.350
A single Bitcoin transaction,

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.600
that's one purchase, or one sale,

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.600
or one transfer uses the
same amount of electricity

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.840
as the typical US household
uses in more than a month.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:05.630
I think the estimate is 53 days.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:07.290
Wow.
Yeah.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.740
They devise this per
transaction energy cost figure.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.830
And then they extrapolate
Bitcoin's transactional load

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.100
to hundreds of billions per year.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.450
Nic Carter is a partner

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.060
at Castle Island Ventures.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.420
He's written a series
of influential articles

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.000
about Bitcoin and energy.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.640
On the basis of this

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.640
somewhat illusory per
transaction energy cost figure,

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.270
they assume that Bitcoin
will grow rapaciously

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.640
in its energy footprint.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.680
In fact, as the Bitcoin network grows

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.770
to support additional transactions,

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.000
it doesn't require additional energy.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.120
Just as the federal reserve
building's electricity bill

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.230
doesn't increase with
every ATM withdrawal,

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.130
the electricity consumed by mining

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:55.200
isn't used to power
individual transactions.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.150
It's used to secure and administer

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.370
the foundation layer of
Bitcoin's monetary network,

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.520
which can then be extended
almost infinitely.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.330
Bitcoin transactions
and Bitcoin's energy use

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.170
are not really correlated.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.760
An additional marginal
transaction doesn't really

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:14.160
add much energy outlay
to the Bitcoin system.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.910
The energy use by Bitcoin miners

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:19.280
has increased significantly
and it will continue to grow,

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.510
but the media's claims are outlandish.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.240
In 2017,

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.300
Newsweek boldly predicted
that Bitcoin was on track

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.800
to consume all of the
world's energy by 2020.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.810
One of the most commonly cited figures

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.000
comes from a 2018 two-page analysis,

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.140
published in Nature Climate Change

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.390
and trumpeted by The New
York Times and other outlets.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:44.350
Bitcoin's growth could single-handedly

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:45.800
push global temperatures

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.640
above the tipping point of two degrees.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.940
Effectively, it makes some
very questionable assumptions

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.250
about Bitcoin to drive this extrapolation

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:56.490
of Bitcoin's future carbon footprint.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:00.660
I finds this cartoonish figure
that Bitcoin could increase

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.660
the world's temperature
by two plus degrees,

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.260
which is completely inconsistent
with the way Bitcoin works.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.330
Nature Climate Change

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.400
went on to publish three rebuttals,

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.520
pointing out the
implausible assumptions used

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.230
to generate its figure.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.610
Including the same fallacy of calculating

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.750
an energy cost per Bitcoin transaction.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:23.170
And then assuming a linear
increase as the network grows.

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.770
So how much energy does Bitcoin
mining actually consume?

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.830
The Cambridge Center
for Alternative Finance

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.900
estimates Bitcoin uses just over

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.070
a hundred terawatt-hours per year,

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:34.600
which is less than gold mining,

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.350
and many other residential,
and industrial activities.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.410
Critics routinely invoke
the idea that Bitcoin

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:42.210
uses more electricity than whole countries

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.530
to generate attention-grabbing headlines,

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:47.130
but that's also true of many industries.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:48.530
And if Bitcoin were a country,

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:51.680
it would rank 32nd out of 59 tracked

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.120
by the Cambridge Center.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.450
And for what it's worth,

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.030
Bitcoin's market cap is
over a trillion dollars,

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:00.090
far more than the GDP
of many of the countries

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.330
to which it's compared.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:04.930
Critics also tend to ignore
that miners are incentivized

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.500
to use energy that would
otherwise go to waste.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:08.570
That's because electricity

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:10.720
is hard to transport over long distances,

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:12.710
while Bitcoin mining can happen anywhere

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.250
that there's internet access.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.770
So miners gravitate toward energy sources

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:18.400
with excess capacity.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.556
They will go to the Amazon,
they will go to the Congo,

00:04:20.556 --> 00:04:22.872
they'd Siberia, they'd go Antarctica,

00:04:22.872 --> 00:04:23.890
or in the middle of the ocean;

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:25.690
they'll go wherever
the cheapest energy is.

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:27.980
Alex Gladstein is chief strategy officer

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:29.650
at the Human Rights Foundation.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:31.990
He points out that
Bitcoin miners lose money

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.960
in competitive electricity markets

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.370
because the cost of the power

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:38.010
they consume exceeds their earnings.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.700
Bitcoin miners can only
pay two to five cents

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.170
per kilowatt for the energy.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:46.170
Industrial nations
consumers pay 10, 15 cents.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.490
And in developing countries,
they pay 20 to 40 cents.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:51.450
What I'm concluding here
with is that Bitcoin miners

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:54.350
need energy that nobody else wants.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:55.810
In the Western United States,

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.890
mobile Bitcoin miners are
already running on electricity

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.070
derived from unused
natural gas from oil wells

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:03.380
that can't be captured

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:05.430
because there are no
pipelines to carry it.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.330
argues that Bitcoin can be used

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:09.610
as a spur to develop more renewable energy

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:12.170
in underdeveloped
countries, remote locations,

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.370
and even existing landfills.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:17.000
A recent industry survey
put Bitcoin mining's

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:20.360
sustainable energy use at around 56%,

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:21.850
a figure that will likely grow.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:23.910
Bitcoin miners are like a sponge.

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.840
Any excess energy bubbling to the point

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.200
where it becomes cheap enough for them,

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.110
they will soak it up.

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:30.530
There are computers all
over the world right now

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:34.790
spitting out random
numbers around the clock

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:39.790
in a competition to try
to solve a useless puzzle.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.920
The claim that Bitcoin mining is useless

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.710
is the essence of the
government's case against Bitcoin

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.280
because it's this component

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:48.570
that directly challenges state power.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:49.790
The work being carried out

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:51.560
by this global computer network

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.630
is what allows Bitcoin to be controlled

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:56.690
by mathematical rules
instead of human actors

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:59.470
vulnerable to government
or corporate control.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.660
Bitcoin is a vote of no confidence

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:05.140
in the monetary and financial
system that exists today.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:07.630
It's a pretty exclusionary system

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:11.110
where we are extremely
beholden to the opinions

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:14.160
of a half dozen individuals
who all think the same way.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:15.330
Would you go so far as to say

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.850
it's almost like a generational movement

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.890
against the status quo,

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:23.460
in favor of a non-inflationary,
part currency?

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:26.020
Absolutely. And it's not
just non-inflationary.

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:29.210
It is a non-discretionary monetary rule.

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:30.920
There's no central banker that can come in

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:32.550
and alter the rules

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.340
and privilege one certain set of society

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:37.020
at the expense of another.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:40.187
That is the core of the movement here.

